      "title": "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "1ef8f231-5b64-5258-a4f9-26b74ba6e889",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny\\prime=\\frac{2 y}{x},\\qquad x\\ne 0\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)=- 5 x^{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f\\prime(x)=- 10 x^{3}.$\n- **II.** Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement.\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=- 5 x^{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\prime(x)&=\\left(- 5 x^{4}\\right)\\prime \\\\ &=-5\\cdot 4 x^{3} \\\\ &=- 20 x^{3} \\\\ &\\not\\equiv - 10 x^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Substituting $y=- 5 x^{4}$ and $y\\prime=- 20 x^{3}$ into the original equation gives expressions that are not identical:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\prime &\\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2 y}{x} \\\\ - 20 x^{3} &\\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\left(- 5 x^{4}\\right)}{x} \\\\ - 20 x^{3} &\\not\\equiv - 10 x^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the function $y=- 5 x^{4}$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "62e6cae4-b660-54b5-958d-781de67e0763",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
